7592186418993 #edb/ref 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{2}^{n+1}>n+1\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1+1}=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+1}>k+1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+2}>k+2.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{(k+1)+1} & ={2}^{k+1}\\cdot {2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {2}^{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {{{2}^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{>\\,k+1} & >2(k+1) \\\\  & =2k+2 \\\\  & =k+2+{{k}_{⏟}}_{>\\,0} \\\\  & >k+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182453" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\ge {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}+3{k}^{2}+5k+2 & \\ge {{field-10}} \\\\  & ={{field-11}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\ge {{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ is true for all\n{{field-13}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea263a27-2e68-51ff-b5e7-5e8c84ebe1bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186418994 #edb/ref 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}+{1}^{2}=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}\\ge 2k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2}\\ge 2(k+1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2} & =({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1)+({k}^{2}+2k+1) \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+{k}^{2}+3{k}^{2}+5k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 2k}+{{3{k}^{2}+5k}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+2 & \\ge 2k+2=2(k+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
